WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.440
They're really clever. They have
these amazing cognitive abilities.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.360
They're more like feathered apes.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.040
This is Betty, she's a female raven.
She's the biggest of the corvids.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.320
And corvids in general are
very, very intelligent birds.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.360
They can creatively problem-solve.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.800
So they can put together sequences
or behaviours that they've learned

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.720
into a novel way to solve a problem.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.360
You see she'll look at things,
she'll cock her head,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.440
and you can actually
see her working it out.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.000
What is it?
Can I benefit anything from that?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.960
The hard part is trying to stay
one step in front of her.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.960
So there's a classic military line
that says improvise, adapt, overcome.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.760
And that's a really positive way
of thinking about anything.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.880
It's about trial and error and
the resilience to keep going.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.680
New Caledonian crows
create multi-step tools,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.120
which are essentially tools that
are strong and thick at one end

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.960
and thin and flexible
at the other end.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.040
And that means that they are able to
put those tools easily into crevices

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.840
and extract food items.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.080
Albert Einstein described
compound interest

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.720
as the eighth wonder of the world.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.320
Crows really understand
compound interest.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.120
So when they save that bit of food
and bury it in the grass,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.200
they know that over time
maggots will come in

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.360
and, from the crow's perspective,
they will have even more food.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.120
It's found its food,
it's saved its food,

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.000
and now it's buried it
in the ground somewhere

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.360
where it gets compound
interest on its investment.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.960
She's burying food in the summer,
you've got files, flies lay eggs,

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.320
maggots hatch out the eggs.
So when she goes back to the food,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.920
not only has she got the rotten bit
of food she's cached anyway,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.880
but she's doubled it in size by
having all the maggots on it as well.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.680
For a long time it was thought
that delayed gratification

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.680
was something that was unique
to humans and non-human great apes

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.840
and some primates,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.720
but it has been found that
corvids will delay gratification.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.560
So they will deny themselves
an immediate reward

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.080
in order to get a reward
of a better quality in the future.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.680
They form really
long-term relationships,

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.920
and those relationships
are themselves

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.760
an example of delayed gratification.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.840
Crows have absolutely nailed
delayed gratification.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.480
There's a seminal book, called The
Marshmallow Test, by Walter Mischel,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.040
which studies young children
and as they've grown up

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.920
and their ability to
delay gratification,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.920
their ability to look at
a marshmallow and wait,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.160
and if they wait long
enough they will get two.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.920
What that study showed is that those
children were able, later on in life,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.960
to be better at managing money,
better at managing relationships,

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.600
healthier, more likely to do
exercise, more likely to eat well.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.840
So delayed gratification is the key

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.320
to putting off those short-term
gains for long-term benefits.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.640
No. No.

